              Black and white: craft defines fine art, not color.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <a href={`/Galleries/Painterly-Fine-Art-Photography/Facing-History/Wild-West/Western-Narratives/Color/${image4.id}`}>
              <img 
                src={image4.src} 
                alt={image4.alt}
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem; color:#666; margin-top:0.5rem; font-style:italic;">
              Presence and tradition through intentional vision.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: What Western Fine Art Is Not -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>What Western Fine Art Is Not</h2>
        </div>

        <p>To understand what qualifies as Western fine art, it helps to clarify what does not:</p>

        <p><strong>Décor art</strong><br>
        Work created primarily to match interior color schemes or fill wall space.</p>

        <p><strong>Tourist art</strong><br>
        Imagery optimized for mass appeal or souvenir value.</p>

        <p><strong>Documentary-only photography</strong><br>
        Work whose primary function is record rather than authored interpretation.</p>

        <p><strong>Genre clichés</strong><br>
        Visual tropes used as shorthand rather than narrative elements.</p>

        <p>The dividing line is intent.</p>

        <p>If the work exists primarily to sell quickly, appeal broadly, or reinforce expectation, it is not fine art.</p>

        <p>Western fine art exists to be considered over time—returning meaning rather than immediate gratification.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Why the Distinction Matters -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>Why the Distinction Matters</h2>
        </div>

        <p>For collectors, the distinction determines long-term value—financially and culturally. Fine art appreciates, enters collections, and becomes part of institutional and private histories.</p>

        <p>For curators, it allows Western art to be presented as serious contemporary subject matter, rather than nostalgic decoration.</p>

        <p>And for artists, the distinction is existential. To be classified as fine art is to participate in a tradition rather than be dismissed by it.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Explore -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>Explore Western Fine Art at K4 Studios</h2>
        </div>

        <p>K4 Studios approaches Western fine art as a cross-medium standard first and a photographic practice second. The work does not mimic painting or photography—it aims to meet the standard that defines Western fine art regardless of medium.</p>

        <p>Explore curated collections that engage the American West with authorship, restraint, and artistic intent:</p>

        <ul style="list-style:disc; padding-left:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
            <a href="/Western-Fine-Art-Photography" style="color:#8b5a2b; font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline;">Western Fine Art Photography</a> — The primary collection and hub
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
            <a href="/Galleries/Painterly-Fine-Art-Photography/Facing-History/Western-Cowboy-Portraits/Color" style="color:#8b5a2b; font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline;">Cowboy & Western Life</a> — Frontier iconography as fine art subject
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
            <a href="/Galleries/Painterly-Fine-Art-Photography/Landscapes" style="color:#8b5a2b; font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline;">Western Landscapes</a> — The land as compositional study
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>Each collection treats the West not as backdrop, but as subject—worthy of the same artistic seriousness applied to any other genre of fine art.</p>

      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- Western Art Series Navigation -->
